coverage_aware.answer:
You may not use GitHub Actions as a general compute or delivery service. Under the Actions terms, Actions should not be used for:

- Cryptomining [S1];
- Disrupting, gaining, or attempting to gain unauthorized access to any service, device, data, account, or network (other than those authorized by the GitHub Bug Bounty program) [S1];
- Providing a stand-alone or integrated application or service offering Actions or its elements for commercial purposes [S1];
- Any activity that places a disproportionate burden on GitHub's servers relative to the benefits to users — for example, don't use Actions as a content delivery network or as part of a serverless application, though a low-benefit Action could be acceptable if it is also low-burden [S1]; or
- If using GitHub-hosted runners, any other activity unrelated to the production, testing, deployment, or publication of the software project associated with the repository where Actions are used [S1].

These restrictions apply regardless of whether an Action uses self-hosted runners [S1]. Actions and its elements also may not be used in violation of the Agreement, the GitHub Acceptable Use Policies, or the Actions service limitations in the Actions documentation [S1]. You may only access and use GitHub Actions to develop and test your application(s), and only one licensed user may access a virtual machine provided by Actions at any time [S1].

To prevent violations and abuse, GitHub may monitor your use of GitHub Actions [S1]. Misuse may result in termination of jobs, restrictions on your ability to use GitHub Actions, disabling of repositories created to run Actions in a way that violates these Terms, or in some cases suspension or termination of your GitHub account [S1].

The provided evidence does not specify any additional enforcement consequences beyond those listed above.

